    "id": "train-1110",
    "question": "A doctor needs to give medicine to a child, and he knows that he cannot give the same amount of medicine that he would give to an adult. The instructions say that for every kilogram of weight the child must be given 5 ml of medicine. The doctor knows that the child weighs 30 kilograms and that also the full dose has to be given in 3 equal parts. How many milligrams will each part of the dose be?",
    "gold": "50",
    "answer": "The total number of ml needed is 30 kg x 5 ml/kg = <<30*5=150>>150 ml\nEach dose has to be 150 ml / 3 doses = <<150/3=50>>50 ml/dose\n#### 50"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine how many milligrams each part of the dose will be, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the total amount of medicine needed for the child.\n2. Determine the amount of medicine per part of the dose.\n3. Convert the amount of medicine per part from milliliters to milligrams.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the total amount of medicine needed for the child.**\n\nThe instructions say that for every kilogram of weight, the child must be given 5 ml of medicine. The child weighs 30 kilograms. Therefore, the total amount of medicine needed is:\n\\[ 30 \\text{ kg} \\times 5 \\text{ ml/kg} = 150 \\text{ ml} \\]\n\n**Step 2: Determine the amount of medicine per part of the dose.**\n\nThe full dose has to be given in 3 equal parts. Therefore, the amount of medicine per part is:\n\\[ \\frac{150 \\text{ ml}}{3} = 50 \\text{ ml} \\]\n\n**Step 3: Convert the amount of medicine per part from milliliters to milligrams.**\n\nSince 1 ml is equivalent to 1000 milligrams, 50 ml is equivalent to:\n\\[ 50 \\text{ ml} \\times 1000 \\text{ mg/ml} = 50000 \\text{ mg} \\]\n\nThus, each part of the dose will be \\(\\boxed{50000}\\) milligrams.",
